 $\mathbf{c}^* L_f(A, E) \mathbf{b} = \langle L_f(A,\mathbf{c} \mathbf{b}^*), E \rangle$.

ref_dict.thm:unb_comp:
\begin{thmy}
\label{thm:unb_comp}
    Fix an arbitrary lattice $\LA$ and let $N_{f_\LA}$ denote the Newton's method of an odd or even elliptic function $f_\LA$ for which $\Poles(\fLA)=\LA$. 
    If $U$     is an unbounded Fatou component, then $U$ is a periodic Baker domain or an escaping wandering domain. Furthermore, $U$ is simply connected.
\end{thmy}

ref_dict.thm:conn_gen:
\begin{thmy}
    \label{thm:conn_gen}
    Fix an arbitrary lattice $\LA$ and let $N_{\fLA}$ denote the Newton's method of an odd or even elliptic function $\fLA$ for which $\Poles(\fLA)=\LA$. If $N_{\fLA}$ has no Herman rings, then its Julia set is connected. 
\end{thmy}

ref_dict.thm:NoHR:
\begin{thmy}\label{thm:NoHR}
    Let $\LA$ be a triangular lattice. Then, for any $b\in \bbC$, 
    the Newton map
    \[N_b(z) = z - \frac{\WLA(z)+b}{\WLA'(z)}\]
    does not have cycles of Herman rings of any period and every Fatou component of $N_b$ is bounded. Furthermore, its Julia set is connected.
\end{thmy}

ref_dict.thm:wandering:
\begin{thmy}\label{thm:wandering}
For any given lattice $\LA$, let $N_{\fLA}$ denote the Newton's method for an odd or even elliptic function $\fLA$. Consider the function $F_{\la}(z)=N_{\fLA}(z)+\la$ for some fixed $\la\in \LA^*$ and let $q\geq 1$. If $F_{\la}$ has a $q$-cycle of (super)attracting  basins containing only free critical points,  then $N_{\fLA}$ has (at least) $2q$ distinct orbits of escaping wandering domains that coexist with the invariant (super)attracting basins for the roots of $\fLA$.
\end{thmy}

post_theorem_intro_text:
We briefly describe the strategy of the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:conn_gen}: provided that the Fatou set, denoted by $\mathcal{F}(N_{f_\Lambda})$, contains no Herman rings, we show that any other Fatou component is simply connected. For otherwise, if $U\subset\mathcal{F}(N_{f_\Lambda})$ is multiply connected, the application of several results from \cite{BFJK2}  implies the existence of a \emph{weakly repelling} fixed point in a bounded complementary component of $U$. The hypothesis on the poles of $f_\Lambda$ then shows that $U$ is unbounded and the last step reduces to proving that $U$ is a simply connected Baker domain or a simply connected escaping wandering domain, which is the essence of our second main result.

\begin{thmy}
\label{thm:unb_comp}
    Fix an arbitrary lattice $\Lambda$ and let $N_{f_\Lambda}$ denote the Newton's method of an odd or even elliptic function $f_\Lambda$ for which $\Poles(f_\Lambda)=\Lambda$. 
    If $U$     is an unbounded Fatou component, then $U$ is a periodic Baker domain or an escaping wandering domain. Furthermore, $U$ is simply connected.
\end{thmy}

We now turn to discuss the realisation of Herman rings, Baker domains and wandering domains. The hypothesis in Theorem \ref{thm:conn_gen} on the absence of Herman rings is addressed for the Newton's method of a one-parameter family of (even) elliptic functions given by
\[\wp_{\Lambda, b}(z)=\wp_\Lambda(z)+b,\]
where $b\in\mathbb{C}$ and $\Lambda$ is triangular, however other results for arbitrary lattices are also given. The Newton maps associated with $\wp_{\Lambda,b}$ are simply denoted by $N_b$. When $N_b$ is restricted to a fundamental parallelogram $\mathcal{Q}$, it has four free, simple critical points unless $\Lambda$ is triangular; in this case, $N_b|\mathcal{Q}$ has exactly two free, double critical points. 
 For any $q\geq 1$, we show that if a $q$-cycle of Herman rings is realised by $N_b$ and $\Lambda$ is arbitrary, then the cycle presents a \emph{nested configuration} and each Herman ring surrounds the same pole; see Proposition~\ref{prop:onepole}. And although Newton's method of an elliptic function $f_\Lambda\in\mathcal{A}(\Lambda)$ may realise unbounded components as established in Theorem~\ref{thm:unb_comp}, exploiting the properties of triangular lattices, we are able to prove our third main result.

\begin{thmy}\label{thm:NoHR}
    Let $\Lambda$ be a triangular lattice. Then, for any $b\in \mathbb{C}$, 
    the Newton map
    \[N_b(z) = z - \frac{\wp_\Lambda(z)+b}{\wp_\Lambda'(z)}\]
    does not have cycles of Herman rings of any period and every Fatou component of $N_b$ is bounded. Furthermore, its Julia set is connected.
\end{thmy}

Our final result is related to the examples constructed by Florido and Fagella in \cite{FF25} on the existence of wandering domains and (super)attracting basins of roots in the same dynamical plane of $N_{f_\Lambda}$ and $f_\Lambda\in\mathcal{A}(\Lambda)$ with $\Lambda$ an arbitrary lattice.

\begin{thmy}\label{thm:wandering}
For any given lattice $\Lambda$, let $N_{f_\Lambda}$ denote the Newton's method for an odd or even elliptic function $f_\Lambda$. Consider the function $F_{\lambda}(z)=N_{f_\Lambda}(z)+\lambda$ for some fixed $\lambda\in \Lambda^*$ and let $q\geq 1$. If $F_{\lambda}$ has a $q$-cycle of (super)attracting  basins containing only free critical points,  then $N_{f_\Lambda}$ has (at least) $2q$ distinct orbits of escaping wandering domains that coexist with the invariant (super)attracting basins for the roots of $f_\Lambda$.
\end{thmy}

In contrast with the lifting method used in \cite{FF25} to generate wandering domains, the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:wandering} rests on the $\Lambda$-invariance property of $N_{f_\Lambda}$ and Tsantaris result in \cite[Theorem 1.5]{Tsa}, which shows that any two commuting transcendental meromorphic functions with infinitely many prepoles share the same Julia set. Surprisingly, the dynamical plane of Newton maps $N_b$ may also exhibit (bounded) wandering domains together with the (super)attracting basins of the roots of $\wp_\Lambda+b$; see Proposition~\ref{ex:wanderN_b} where we describe parameters that give rise to pairs of distinct orbits of wandering domains. Theorem~\ref{thm:NoHR} implies these wandering domains are simply connected.

The proofs of Theorem~\ref{thm:conn_gen} and Theorem~\ref{thm:unb_comp} are given in Section~\ref{sec::Conn}, while in Section~\ref{sec:ParmFam}, the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:NoHR} is presented. 
 Section~\ref{sec:wan} contains the proofs of Theorem~\ref{thm:wandering} and Proposition~\ref{ex:wanderN_b}, including some numerical experiments.

ref_dict.thm full Fourier expansion:
\begin{thm}\label{thm full Fourier expansion}
Suppose $\ell> n$, $D\equiv 3\modulo 4$ and $n\equiv 2\modulo 4$. 
The constant term along $\bff{Z}$ of the Eisenstein series   
$E_\ell(g, s=\ell+1)$ has the Fourier expansion 
\begin{align*}
   E_{\ell,0}(g)+\sum_{\substack{0\neq T\in\bff{V}_{0}\\\lrangle{T}{T}\geq 0}}a_{T}(g_{f})\call{W}_{T}(g_{\infty}),\,g=g_{f}g_{\infty}\in\bff{G}(\A_{f})\times \bff{G}(\R),
\end{align*}
where the Fourier coefficients satisfy:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The locally constant functions $a_{T}(g_{f})$ are valued in $\Q$ when restricted to $g_{f}\in\bff{M}(\A_{f})$.
    \item The $\bff{N}$-constant term $E_{\ell,0}(g)$ is a rational multiple of $|\nu(g)|_{\A_{E}}^{\ell+1}\frac{\zeta_{E}(\ell+1)}{\pi^{2\ell+1}}[u_{1}^{n}][u_{2}^{n}]$ when restricted to $g\in \bff{M}(\A)$.
\end{itemize}
Moreover, when $g_{f}=1$, 
$E_{\ell}(g_{\infty},s=\ell+1)$ have the following explicit Fourier expansion:
\begin{align*}
   E_{\ell,0}(g_\infty)+C_{\ell}\sum_{0\neq T\in\bff{V}_{0}(\Z)\atop \lrangle{T}{T}=0}\sigma_{E,\ell}(T)\call{W}_{T}(g_{\infty})
    +D_{n,\ell}\sum_{T\in\bff{V}_{0}(\Z)\atop \lrangle{T}{T}>0}\left(\prod_{p|\lrangle{T}{T}}Q_{T,p}(p^{\ell-\frac{n-1}{2}})\right)\call{W}_{T}(g_{\infty}),
\end{align*}
where
\[C_{\ell}=\frac{(-1)^{\ell}2^{2\ell+1}}{(\ell!)^{2}}\quad \text{and} \quad D_{n,\ell}=\frac{2^{2n+2}(2\ell-n+2)D^{\ell+1-\frac{n}{2}}}{(\ell!)^{2}\left|B_{2\ell-n+2}\right|\sigma_{\ell+1-\frac{n}{2}}(D)} \in \Bbb Q.\]
\end{thm}

expanded_theorem:
\label{main theorem} We first prove the following theorem. \begin{thm}\label{thm full Fourier expansion}
Suppose $\ell> n$, $D\equiv 3\modulo 4$ and $n\equiv 2\modulo 4$. 
The constant term along $\bff{Z}$ of the Eisenstein series   
$E_\ell(g, s=\ell+1)$ has the Fourier expansion 
\begin{align*}
   E_{\ell,0}(g)+\sum_{\substack{0\neq T\in\bff{V}_{0}\\\lrangle{T}{T}\geq 0}}a_{T}(g_{f})\call{W}_{T}(g_{\infty}),\,g=g_{f}g_{\infty}\in\bff{G}(\A_{f})\times \bff{G}(\R),
\end{align*}
where the Fourier coefficients satisfy:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The locally constant functions $a_{T}(g_{f})$ are valued in $\Q$ when restricted to $g_{f}\in\bff{M}(\A_{f})$.
    \item The $\bff{N}$-constant term $E_{\ell,0}(g)$ is a rational multiple of $|\nu(g)|_{\A_{E}}^{\ell+1}\frac{\zeta_{E}(\ell+1)}{\pi^{2\ell+1}}[u_{1}^{n}][u_{2}^{n}]$ when restricted to $g\in \bff{M}(\A)$.
\end{itemize}
Moreover, when $g_{f}=1$, 
$E_{\ell}(g_{\infty},s=\ell+1)$ have the following explicit Fourier expansion:
\begin{align*}
   E_{\ell,0}(g_\infty)+C_{\ell}\sum_{0\neq T\in\bff{V}_{0}(\Z)\atop \lrangle{T}{T}=0}\sigma_{E,\ell}(T)\call{W}_{T}(g_{\infty})
    +D_{n,\ell}\sum_{T\in\bff{V}_{0}(\Z)\atop \lrangle{T}{T}>0}\left(\prod_{p|\lrangle{T}{T}}Q_{T,p}(p^{\ell-\frac{n-1}{2}})\right)\call{W}_{T}(g_{\infty}),
\end{align*}
where
\[C_{\ell}=\frac{(-1)^{\ell}2^{2\ell+1}}{(\ell!)^{2}}\quad \text{and} \quad D_{n,\ell}=\frac{2^{2n+2}(2\ell-n+2)D^{\ell+1-\frac{n}{2}}}{(\ell!)^{2}\left|B_{2\ell-n+2}\right|\sigma_{\ell+1-\frac{n}{2}}(D)} \in \Bbb Q.\]
\end{thm}
Assume that the discriminant of $E$ is odd. When $\ell>n$, the Eisenstein series $E_\ell(g)$ has rational Fourier coefficients in the following sense:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The locally constant functions $a_{T}(E_{\ell},g_{f})$ take values in $\mathbb{Q}$ when restricted to $\mathbf{M}(\A_{f})$.
    \item The constant term $E_{\ell,\mathbf{N}}(g)$ is a rational multiple of $|\nu(g)|_{\A_{E}}^{\ell+1}\cdot\frac{\zeta_{E}(\ell+1)}{\pi^{2\ell+1}}[u_{1}^{n}][u_{2}^{n}]$ when restricted to $\mathbf{M}(\mathbb{A})$, where $[u_{1}^{n}][u_{2}^{n}]$ denotes a vector in a fixed basis of $\mathbb{V}_{\ell}$.
\end{itemize}
Moreover, the value of the $T$th Fourier coefficient $a_{T}(E_{\ell},g_{f})$ at $g_{f}=1$ is non-zero if and only if $T$ lies in a self-dual Hermitian $\oo_{E}$-lattice $\mathbf{V}_{0}(\mathbb{Z})$ of $\mathbf{V}_{0}$. We have an explicit formula for the Fourier coefficients $a_{T}(E_{\ell},1)$, and their denominators are all divisors of an integer depending only on $E,\ell$, and $n$.

theorem.content:
[Counterexample to the Berger--Coburn conjecture]\label{thm:main}
There exists a measurable symbol $g$ on $\mathbb C^n$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $gk_a\in L^2(d\mu)$ for every $a\in\mathbb C^n$;
\item the Toeplitz operator $T_g$ extends to a bounded operator on $H^2(\mathbb C^n,d\mu)$;
\item the heat transform $g^{(1/4)}$ is unbounded on $\mathbb C^n$.
\end{enumerate}

