post_theorem_intro_text:
This direct approach to the diagram of Griffiths (\ref{Commutative_diagram_Kodaira_Spencer_map_dP_intro}) is not published yet and yields a both computational and much explicit perspective. Given the assumptions of the theorem we easily deduce
\begin{align*}
	\ker(d \mathcal{P}_{B,f}^k) = \ker(d \mathcal{P}_{B,f}^1), \quad k \geq 1
\end{align*}
(Corollary \ref{corollary_conjecture_ker_dphi_f_k}). Let $g = g_{\Gamma}(f) \cdot x^w \in \ker(d \mathcal{P}_f^1)$. Then we get the distinction
\begin{equation} \label{distinction_scalar_product_0_minus1_getminus2}
	\begin{tikzcd}
		\langle w,n_{\Gamma} \rangle = \left\{\begin{array}{lll} 0, & \Leftrightarrow g \equiv 0 \\
			-1, &  \Leftrightarrow g \in \ker(\kappa_f) \\
			\leq -2, & \quad \, \, \textrm{exceptional cases}
		\end{array}\right.
	\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
and $\langle w,n_{\Gamma'} \rangle \geq 0$ for $\Gamma' \neq \Gamma$ by Theorem (\ref{Theorem_formula_ker_ITT_intro}) with $n_{\Gamma}$ the inner facet normal to $\Gamma$. The last case in the distinction causes $\Phi_{f \vert{Im \, \kappa_f}}$ to be not injective, which implies in particular that the infinitesimal Torelli theorem (ITT) fails for such hypersurfaces $Y_f$. \leavevmode \\ \\
For smooth projective hypersurfaces $Y_f \subset \mathbb{P}^n$ of degree $\geq n+2$, where $n \geq 3$ the Kodaira-Spencer map $\kappa_f$ is surjective and we use this to get a new proof of the ITT for such nondegenerate hypersurfaces. We then finish with the following corollary
\begin{corollar}
	Given $n=3$ assume $\Int(\Delta) \cap M \nsubseteq \textrm{plane}$. Then
\[  \ker(\Phi_{f \vert{Im \, \kappa_f}}) = \{ 0  \},  \]
We say that $Y_f$ fulfills the ITT for $\mathcal{X} \rightarrow B$.  
\end{corollar}

This corollary follows easily from above: Namely we construct a $3$-dimensional empty polytope $Q$ with $6$ vertices. $Q$ is the convex span of an empty triangle with vertex $(0,0,0)$ and this triangle dilated by $w$. Then we use a theorem of White on empty $3$-simplices to deduce $|Q \cap M| \geq 7$ under the assumption $\langle w,n_{\Gamma} \rangle \leq -2$, contradiction.
\leavevmode 
\\ \\
Our article relies on results from (\cite{DK86}) and (\cite{Bat93}). Let us list common methods with the authors V.I. Danilov, A.G. Khovanskii and V.V. Batyrev:
\begin{itemize}
	\item We reduce the calculation of the Hodge component $H^{n-k,k-1}(Y_f, \mathbb{C})$ to the calculation of (\ref{equation_mixed_Hodge_components_of_Z_f_of_min_wei}) (similarly to \cite{DK86}), see section \ref{Section_cohomology_affine_hypersurface_nonsingular_compactification}.
	\item We follow \cite{Bat93} to define the \textit{jacobian ring} $R_f$ and the \textit{(interior) module} $R_{Int,f}$ over $R_f$ (see section \ref{section_constr_jac_ring}) and of course use the identification (\ref{equation_mixed_Hodge_components_of_Z_f_of_min_wei}).
\end{itemize}

In section (\ref{section_smooth_and_stable_points}) we study $\Delta$ up to replacing by an integral lattice vector. In particular we effort $(0,...,0) \in \Int(\Delta)$  to build a smooth quotient of $B$ by the torus $T$. \leavevmode \\ \\
Concerning the cokernel $\coker(\kappa_{f})$ the situation might get more complicated. In (\cite{Mav03}) there were made some efforts for $Y_f$ a K3 hypersurface (so called non-polynomial deformations), realizing these deformations as deformations of complete intersections in a single big toric variety, but is seems unlikely that this representation is useful in order to compute the missing vector space $\ker(\Phi_{f \vert{\coker(\kappa_f)}})$. In (\cite{BrGr12}) there were made some efforts to combine the Hodge components and their computation to the generic Picard number of the hypersurface.

